    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-old female presents with sudden, excruciating pain, redness, and a dramatic drop in vision in the left eye that began 7 days after undergoing uncomplicated phacoemulsification with intraocular lens implantation. Clinical findings include:<br>• Visual acuity: <b>Hand motion (HM) only</b> (baseline 20/25 postoperative day 1)<br>• Slit-lamp biomicroscopy: <b>Eyelid edema, severe conjunctival injection, chemosis, dense corneal edema, and a 0.6-mm layer of purulent debris in the inferior anterior chamber (hypopyon)</b><br>• Funduscopy: <b>Completely obscured red reflex with a dense vitreal yellow-green haze</b><br><br>What is the most likely diagnosis, and what is the immediate, gold-standard management?', '["Acute angle-closure glaucoma; emergent Nd:YAG laser peripheral iridotomy", "Steroid-induced uveitis; immediate discontinuation of postoperative prednisolone drops and initiation of oral corticosteroids", "Toxic Anterior Segment Syndrome (TASS); immediate hourly topical prednisolone acetate and intensive cycloplegics", "Acute postoperative endophthalmitis; emergent vitreous tap for cultures and intravitreal injection of broad-spectrum antibiotics (Vancomycin and Ceftazidime)", "Posterior capsular rupture with vitreous loss; urgent pars plana vitrectomy with secondary scleral-fixated IOL"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The clinical presentation of acute, severe pain, marked vision loss, conjunctival injection, **hypopyon** (pus in the anterior chamber), and a **loss of the red reflex** (due to vitreous clouding) within 1 week of cataract surgery is highly characteristic of **acute postoperative endophthalmitis**. This is a devastating, sight-threatening medical emergency. The most common causative organism is *Staphylococcus epidermidis* (coagulase-negative staphylococcus) followed by *Staphylococcus aureus* and Gram-negative rods. Immediate management is mandatory and consists of a **vitreous tap** (to obtain fluid for Gram stain and culture) followed immediately by **intravitreal injection of broad-spectrum empiric antibiotics** (typically **Vancomycin** for Gram-positives and **Ceftazidime** or Amikacin for Gram-negatives). If the patient''s visual acuity is Light Perception (LP) or worse, the Endophthalmitis Vitrectomy Study (EVS) guidelines recommend immediate **pars plana vitrectomy (PPV)**.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 61-old gentleman presents with mild eye redness and painless blurry vision in the left eye 16 hours after undergoing uncomplicated cataract surgery. Ophthalmic examination reveals:<br>• Visual acuity: <b>20/80</b><br>• Slit-lamp examination: <b>Diffuse, limbus-to-limbus corneal edema, severe anterior chamber cell and flare, and a small hypopyon</b><br>• Posterior segment: <b>Perfectly clear vitreous gel, bright red reflex, no vitritis</b><br>• Intraocular pressure: <b>13 mmHg</b><br><br>What is the most likely diagnosis, and what is the pathophysiology and treatment of this condition?', '["Acute infectious endophthalmitis; bacterial colonization of the anterior chamber, treated with intravitreal vancomycin", "Posterior capsular opacification; epithelial cell proliferation, treated with Nd:YAG laser capsulotomy", "Irvine-Gass syndrome; inflammatory cystoid macular edema, treated with topical NSAIDs", "Phacolytic glaucoma; leakage of lens proteins through an intact capsule, treated with emergent cataract extraction", "Toxic Anterior Segment Syndrome (TASS); sterile inflammatory reaction to intraoperative contaminants, treated with intensive topical corticosteroids"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Toxic Anterior Segment Syndrome (TASS)** is an acute, sterile, non-infectious anterior segment inflammatory reaction that classically occurs within **12 to 24 hours** after intraocular surgery. It is caused by chemical or toxic contaminants on surgical instruments, cannulas, viscoelastics, or intraocular solutions. Clinical hallmarks include **diffuse, limbus-to-limbus corneal edema** (due to widespread corneal endothelial toxicity) and severe anterior chamber inflammation (cell/flare, sometimes hypopyon), but **crucially, there is no vitritis/vitreal involvement**, and the vitreous remains perfectly clear with a bright red reflex. TASS is typically **painless** or causes only mild discomfort. Unlike infectious endophthalmitis (which occurs later, is severely painful, and