    (v_topic_id, 'A 73-old female presents with mild eye redness and painless blurry vision in the left eye 27 hours after undergoing uncomplicated cataract surgery. Ophthalmic examination reveals:<br>• Visual acuity: <b>20/80</b><br>• Slit-lamp examination: <b>Diffuse, limbus-to-limbus corneal edema, severe anterior chamber cell and flare, and a small hypopyon</b><br>• Posterior segment: <b>Perfectly clear vitreous gel, bright red reflex, no vitritis</b><br>• Intraocular pressure: <b>16 mmHg</b><br><br>What is the most likely diagnosis, and what is the pathophysiology and treatment of this condition?', '["Acute infectious endophthalmitis; bacterial colonization of the anterior chamber, treated with intravitreal vancomycin", "Phacolytic glaucoma; leakage of lens proteins through an intact capsule, treated with emergent cataract extraction", "Toxic Anterior Segment Syndrome (TASS); sterile inflammatory reaction to intraoperative contaminants, treated with intensive topical corticosteroids", "Irvine-Gass syndrome; inflammatory cystoid macular edema, treated with topical NSAIDs", "Posterior capsular opacification; epithelial cell proliferation, treated with Nd:YAG laser capsulotomy"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Toxic Anterior Segment Syndrome (TASS)** is an acute, sterile, non-infectious anterior segment inflammatory reaction that classically occurs within **12 to 24 hours** after intraocular surgery. It is caused by chemical or toxic contaminants on surgical instruments, cannulas, viscoelastics, or intraocular solutions. Clinical hallmarks include **diffuse, limbus-to-limbus corneal edema** (due to widespread corneal endothelial toxicity) and severe anterior chamber inflammation (cell/flare, sometimes hypopyon), but **crucially, there is no vitritis/vitreal involvement**, and the vitreous remains perfectly clear with a bright red reflex. TASS is typically **painless** or causes only mild discomfort. Unlike infectious endophthalmitis (which occurs later, is severely painful, and involves the vitreous), TASS is treated aggressively with **intensive topical corticosteroids** (e.g., prednisolone acetate every 1 hour), which results in rapid resolution of the inflammation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-old male reports a gradual, painless, progressive decline in visual acuity and worsening glare while driving at night in the right eye over the past 6 months. The patient underwent highly successful phacoemulsification with posterior chamber intraocular lens (PCIOL) implantation in that same eye 4 years ago. Clinical examination findings:<br>• Visual acuity: <b>20/300 (was 20/20 at 1-month post-op)</b><br>• Slit-lamp examination: <b>PCIOL is centered and stable in the capsular bag; the posterior capsule is thickened with a granular, ''wrinkled'' appearance and multiple tiny, translucent vesicular lesions (Elschnig pearls)</b><br>• Dilated funduscopy: <b>Clear vitreous; normal optic nerve and macula</b><br><br>What is the most likely diagnosis, and what is the standard, non-invasive outpatient treatment of choice?', '["Posterior capsular opacification (PCO); Nd:YAG laser capsulotomy", "Cystoid macular edema (Irvine-Gass syndrome); intravitreal anti-VEGF injection", "Intraocular lens subluxation; surgical scleral fixation of the PCIOL", "Dry age-related macular degeneration; outpatient argon laser photocoagulation", "Posterior capsular opacification (PCO); surgical explantation and exchange of the intraocular lens"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Posterior capsular opacification (PCO)** is the most common late complication of successful cataract surgery, historically referred to as a ''secondary cataract''. It is caused by the proliferation, migration, and transdifferentiation of residual **lens epithelial cells** (from the equator of the capsular bag) across the posterior capsule. Slit-lamp findings classically demonstrate a wrinkled, opacified posterior capsule or clusters of vesicular structures known as **Elschnig pearls**. The standard, highly effective, non-invasive treatment is an outpatient **Nd:YAG laser capsulotomy**, which uses a photodisruptive laser to create a small central opening in the posterior capsule, restoring a clear visual axis and immediately improving visual acuity.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 60-old gentleman complains of progressive, painless blurring of central vision in the left eye that began 5 weeks after undergoing uncomplicated cataract surgery. The patient reports that straight lines appear slightly wavy (metamorphopsia). Clinical examination findings:<br>• Visual acuity: <b>Hand motion (HM) only (was 20/20 at 1-week postoperative visit)</b><br>• Slit-lamp examination: <b>Anterior segment is quiet; PCIOL is stable in the capsular bag; posterior capsule is clear</b><br>• Dilated funduscopy: <b>Loss of the normal foveal reflex with a subtle, thickened, cystic appearance of the macula</b><br>• Optical coherence tomography (OCT): <b>Marked retinal thickening with intraretinal cystic fluid spaces in a flower-petal pattern</b><br><br>What is the most likely diagnosis, and what is the standard first-line management?', '["Central retinal vein occlusion (CRVO); immediate panretinal photocoagulation (PRP)", "Posterior capsular opacification (PCO); Nd:YAG laser capsulotomy", "Acute postoperative endophthalmitis; emergent vitreous tap and intravitreal vancomycin", "Wet age-related macular degeneration; emergent photodynamic therapy with verteporfin", "Cystoid macular edema (Irvine-Gass syndrome); topical NSAID drops (e.g., ketorolac) combined with topical corticosteroid drops (e.g., prednisolone)"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Cystoid Macular Edema (CME)** occurring after cataract surgery is known as **Irvine-Gass syndrome**. It typically peaks around **4 to 10 weeks** postoperatively. The pathophysiology involves surgical manipulation triggering the release of inflammatory mediators (prostaglandins) that diffuse posteriorly, causing a breakdown of the blood-retinal barrier and subsequent fluid accumulation in the Henle outer plexiform layer of the macula. OCT is highly diagnostic, classically demonstrating **intraretinal cystic spaces** arranged in a petaloid or flower-petal pattern. The standard first-line treatment is medical, combining **topical NSAIDs** (to block prostaglandin synthesis) with **topical corticosteroids** (to suppress the inflammatory pathway), which achieves resolution in the vast majority of cases.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 9-week-old infant is brought to the pediatrician due to persistent vomiting, poor feeding, and failure to thrive since birth. On physical examination, the infant is found to be lethargic, jaundiced, and has prominent hepatosplenomegaly. Urinalysis reveals the presence of reducing substances, but glucose oxidase test is negative. Red blood cell enzyme assay confirms a severe deficiency of galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltransferase (GALT). Ophthalmic screening with a red reflex exam reveals bilateral, central lens opacities with an ''oil-droplet'' appearance. What is the diagnosis, what substance is accumulating within the lens to cause cataracts, and what is the primary management?', '["Classic galactosemia; accumulation of galactitol in the lens; immediate dietary elimination of lactose and galactose", "Alport syndrome; defective type IV collagen synthesis in the lens capsule; close monitoring and annual screening", "Classic galactosemia; accumulation of sorbitol in the lens; immediate dietary supplementation with glucose", "Galactokinase deficiency; accumulation of galactose-1-phosphate in the lens; oral administration of lactase enzyme", "Congenital rubella syndrome; systemic viral replication in the lens epithelium; immediate surgical lens extraction"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This infant presents with **classic galactosemia**, an autosomal recessive disorder caused by a deficiency in **galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltransferase (GALT)**. The accumulation of galactose-1-phosphate leads to multi-system toxicity (hepatomegaly, jaundice, vomiting, renal tubulopathy, and Escherichia coli sepsis). In the lens of the eye, excess galactose is converted by the enzyme **aldose reductase** into **galactitol**, a sugar alcohol. Galactitol cannot diffuse out of the lens, creating an osmotic gradient that draws water in, leading to swelling, rupture of lens fibers, and the development of characteristic **''oil-droplet'' cataracts**. The primary management is the **immediate and lifelong dietary elimination of lactose and galactose** (milk/dairy products). If initiated early, the cataracts and liver damage can regress.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-old man presents with a 4-year history of progressive, painless blurring of vision in both eyes. The patient also complains of generalized muscle weakness and notes that he has difficulty releasing his grip after shaking hands or holding a doorknob. Physical examination reveals bilateral ptosis, expressionless facial features (''myopathic facies''), temporalis muscle wasting, and frontal balding. Slit-lamp examination of both lenses demonstrates multiple polychromatic, highly iridescent red, green, and gold needle-like crystals in the deep cortex and subcapsular regions, reminiscent of a ''christmas tree''. What is the most likely diagnosis, and what is the underlying genetic mutation?', '["Myotonic dystrophy type 1 (DM1); CTG trinucleotide repeat expansion in the DMPK gene", "Fabry disease; X-linked recessive alpha-galactosidase A deficiency", "Wilson''s disease; autosomal recessive mutation in the ATP7B copper-transporting gene", "Homocystinuria; cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency with cystine depletion", "Marfan syndrome; autosomal dominant missense mutation in the FBN1 gene"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The clinical combination of progressive cataracts, myotonia (inability to relax muscles after voluntary contraction, e.g., releasing grip), distal muscle weakness, ptosis, and frontal balding is diagnostic of **myotonic dystrophy type 1 (DM1)**. The pathognomonic cataract finding in myotonic dystrophy is the **''christmas-tree'' cataract**, characterized by beautiful, polychromatic, iridescent needle-like crystals (composed of cholesterol and lipids) scattered throughout the lens cortex. DM1 is an autosomal dominant disorder caused by a **CTG trinucleotide repeat expansion** in the 3'' untranslated region of the **DMPK** (dystrophia myotonica protein kinase) gene on chromosome 19. It exhibits genetic anticipation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 73-old male is referred for an ophthalmic examination. Physical examination is notable for tall stature, exceptionally long and slender fingers (arachnodactyly), high-arched palate, and mild pectus excavatum. The patient''s father died suddenly of a ruptured thoracic aortic dissection at age 38. Slit-lamp examination after pupillary dilation reveals that the crystalline lens in both eyes is displaced superotemporally (upward and outward), with several stretched, intact ciliary zonules visible. What is the most likely diagnosis, and what is the inheritance pattern and genetic defect?', '["Homocystinuria; autosomal recessive deficiency of cystathionine beta-synthase", "Marfan syndrome; autosomal recessive defect in the COL3A1 gene encoding Type III Collagen", "Marfan syndrome; autosomal dominant defect in the FBN1 gene encoding Fibrillin-1", "Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (vascular type); autosomal dominant defect in vascular collagen deposition", "Weill-Marchesani syndrome; autosomal dominant defect in the ADAMTS10 gene causing downward subluxation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This patient displays the classic features of **Marfan syndrome**, an autosomal dominant connective tissue disorder caused by mutations in the **FBN1 gene** on chromosome 15, which encodes the glycoprotein **fibrillin-1** (a major component of extracellular microfibrils). Fibrillin-1 is a crucial structural component of the **ciliary zonules** that suspend the lens. The classic ectopia lentis (lens displacement/subluxation) in Marfan syndrome is **superotemporal (upward and outward)**, and the zonules typically remain intact though stretched. This contrasts with **homocystinuria**, an autosomal recessive metabolic disorder (cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency) where lens displacement is classically **inferonasal (downward and inward)**, and the ciliary zonules are completely broken/disrupted, often accompanied by intellectual disability and thromboembolic events.');
